WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.260
 - Hello, everybody, and welcome to chapter one

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:03.400
 of Python for Everybody.

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.280
 I'm Charles Severance, I'm your instructor,

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.760
 and I welcome you to this class.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.040
 The basic goal of this class is to teach everybody

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.200
 how to program, regardless of your background.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.860
 You don't have to be a math whiz,

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:17.840
 you don't have to be a computer expert.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:21.720
 No matter how old you are or what your background is,

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:23.020
 we want to teach you how to program.

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:25.620
 So welcome to the course, welcome to chapter one.

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:31.600
 So the first thing to understand is that

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:33.640
 the purpose to learn to program is because

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:35.340
 computers want to do things for us.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:38.640
 They are built and created and designed,

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.800
 and their hardware is set up so that they

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.740
 basically ask us what do you want to do next.

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:47.500
 If you grab your phone,

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:52.760
 your phone sort of does nothing

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:55.360
 until you tell it what to do.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:58.340
 It waits for you, and it's just waiting for you,

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:01.680
 and all the hardware, computer technology around you

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.800
 is generally waiting for you.

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:06.560
 And we can use this for useful things.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:11.560
 We could play video games, we could have it help navigate

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:15.240
 for our cars, some day we might even have self-driving cars.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:20.240
 And it's really, in a sense, in my mind, silly

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:23.200
 if you spend your whole life not really

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:24.560
 understanding this technology.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:28.240
 And I think it's important that we learn to tell

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:31.320
 these computers what to do, rather than just let them

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.400
 increasingly control our lives.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:37.160
 And so, as we'll see, computers aren't very smart

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:38.760
 on their own, we humans are the ones

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:40.080
 that imbue them with knowledge,

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.000
 and what we need to learn to speak their language.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.360
 It is much easier for us to learn to speak their language

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.520
 than it is for them to learn to speak our language.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:51.240
 Although, with these cell phones, we're starting to see

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:54.120
 little bits where they can begin to understand,

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:57.700
 but you would be amazed at the 40 or 50 years

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:01.400
 that it takes, has taken us to understand,

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:04.700
 how to build programs to begin to understand.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:07.680
 So, I'm bringing you into something

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.820
 where you are going to learn the ways of programming

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:12.720
 and the ways of the computer,

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:15.160
 because it's easier to teach you how to program

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:18.040
 than it is to teach this, how to work in your world.

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:20.380
 Even though, ultimately, the goal is

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:22.700
 to get this to do work for you.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:25.340
 So, part of what I'm trying to do

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:27.560
 is move you from a user perspective,

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:28.960
 where you just look at the computer

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:32.560
 as something that someone else has constructed,

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:35.740
 and you are the user of, to the point

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:36.980
 where you construct new things.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:39.000
 Now, the first kinds of things that you're going to construct

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.500
 are actually things to solve your own problems.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:44.720
 And it's very popular now to work on data,

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:46.880
 and Python is an excellent programming language

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:49.460
 for data mining and data analysis,

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:51.400
 and that's a lot of what we're going to do in this course.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:53.760
 Although, really, it's a gateway to all kinds of things,

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:56.120
 like, you know, artificial intelligence,

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:59.080
 or gaming, or navigation, or mobile applications,

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:01.560
 or entertainment, all kinds of things.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:03.400
 But first, we have to learn to program.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:06.660
 We have to move from using the computer as a tool

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:08.620
 to using the tools within the computer

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:12.480
 that allow us to change how the computer sees the world.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:15.360
 So, there's a couple of reasons

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:16.800
 that you might want to be a programmer.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:20.340
 Some of you are looking to improve your career,

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:22.160
 to be paid to work on programming.

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:24.320
 I've been a paid programmer most of my life,

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:25.720
 and I like it.

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:27.260
 It's a good job.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:30.760
 You don't have to stand in the mud.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:32.740
 You don't have to lift things.

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:33.980
 You have to use your brain.

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:38.000
 And I'll just say that it has been nice for my career

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:39.840
 to not be exposed to the elements,

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:42.200
 but to be able to work often wherever I want.

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:45.300
 But that's actually our secondary goal.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:47.880
 Our first goal is to get you to write programs

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:49.580
 that solve problems that you have to solve.

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:51.960
 Maybe you have a job as an accountant,

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:54.180
 or a lawyer, or something else.

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:56.760
 And maybe you run across some data.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:59.800
 Maybe there's some system that logs your time,

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:02.120
 and it's not quite giving the report that you want to give.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:04.680
 And so you say, could I just grab the log data myself

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:08.000
 and write a program to do some analysis to say,

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.560
 what's the average this versus that,

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:13.360
 or the average of some other thing, right?

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:16.380
 And so, that's the basic idea

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:21.080
 that you'll initially use computers to serve your own ends.

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:22.960
 That makes it a lot easier to write programs,

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:24.800
 because you don't have to worry about

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:26.560
 a million users using your software.

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:28.800
 If it works for you, then we're happy.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:31.160
 And so, it takes a little more training

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:32.820
 to write software for other people,

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:35.940
 or for thousands and thousands of other people.

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:38.480
 And so part of what I want to do

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:41.640
 is I want to change your perspective.

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:44.880
 You look at this from the outside,

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:48.800
 and you see it from the outside, and you click on things.

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:50.280
 I want to turn this around,

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:52.700
 and I want you to be the person inside this

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:54.760
 looking out at the world.

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:57.600
 And as a programmer, we are making things

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:00.560
 inside these computers for the world.

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:04.480
 And so we want to pull you into being part of this.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:08.640
 We want you inside this, or thinking inside this.

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:13.640
 And what you learn is that if you're inside this computer,

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:16.760
 and you are taking your instructions to build programs

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:21.760
 to be used by the human outside the computer,

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:25.640
 you have things that you need to take advantage of.

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:27.640
 There's things like the central processing unit,

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:28.720
 the memory of this system,

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:30.240
 the network connection of this system,

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:33.560
 the disk drive, or permanent storage on this system.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:36.880
 And as a programmer, you are kind of mediating

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:39.800
 between all those internal resources that this has,

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:42.760
 that are not very smart, but highly powerful,

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:45.240
 and mediating with what that user wants, right?

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:48.360
 And so we take the end user, and we programmers,

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:51.800
 we serve the end user, but the computer serves us,

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:55.660
 so together, between us and all the computer's resources,

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:58.640
 we can serve the needs of the end user.

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:03.640
 And we do this by writing code, or programming, okay?

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:05.200
 And what is that?

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:09.280
 Well, programming is a sequence of instructions

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:10.960
 where we are giving instructions

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:13.040
 to the resources inside the computer

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:15.280
 in a way to accomplish the goals of the end user.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:18.780
 And remember, sometimes we are our own end user.

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:23.780
 It's not just, you're not always doing a startup,

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:27.320
 you're not always writing a mobile gaming system.

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:29.160
 Sometimes you're writing something for yourself,

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:30.040
 but that's okay.

00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:36.300
 So sometimes you're writing something to solve a problem,

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:38.640
 you're like crafting, you're doing something

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:41.080
 that you could do by hand or manually,

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:45.680
 and you're making some clever little 25 or 100 line program,

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:48.520
 and you're putting that in.

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:50.560
 Other times, like when I work on

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:53.340
 the open source learning management system, Sakai,

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:54.880
 it is my creativity.

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:58.320
 I've got an idea, and I want to share it with a million users,

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:01.660
 and so I write my code for an external audience.

00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:04.320
 And so code is that sequence of instructions

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:06.600
 that the computer itself doesn't know

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:10.080
 how to hand a roster out, but I can write code

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:12.280
 that will hand a roster out by looking at the data

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:16.200
 that's inside this computer, inside this application.

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:18.440
 And so if you think about programs,

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:22.520
 we have programs for computers and programs for humans.

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:25.120
 And a number of years ago, now I'm,

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:27.700
 sooner or later this will be me showing my age,

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:30.720
 this is an example of the Macarena.

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:33.000
 And the Macarena is a song that effectively

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:34.880
 is a sequence of instructions.

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:37.240
 You put your left hand out, you put your right hand out,

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:39.720
 you put it on the shoulder, you wiggle, wiggle, wiggle,

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:41.400
 and you spin around and you do things.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:46.400
 And this, this is a program for people,

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:49.360
 and so I want you to take a quick look at this

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:52.680
 and see if you can find anything wrong

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:54.180
 with this particular program.

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:56.580
 So look really closely.

00:07:56.580 --> 00:08:04.520
 So I'll show you.

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:09.200
 It's got some typographical errors in it,

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:13.800
 and we as humans are really good at reading

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:16.180
 or hearing typographical errors

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:19.320
 and correcting them automatically and instantly.

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:22.200
 And, but computers are not.

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:24.220
 Computers are extremely literal.

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:29.040
 If it saw this ham instead of hand,

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:31.080
 it would think what's a ham?

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:32.880
 And why am I going to hit someone

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:34.720
 in the back of the head with a ham?

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:37.840
 And why would I take my left hand and hit somebody?

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:39.980
 That's, you know, these are all bad things.

00:08:39.980 --> 00:08:43.480
 But the computer is going to take us very literally.

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:46.720
 And so we have to be really precise.

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:49.520
 And the computer just doesn't know the difference

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:52.900
 between what we mean and what we say.

00:08:52.900 --> 00:08:54.760
 So we have to be very precise.

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:59.440
 And this is one of the great frustrations that people have

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:02.080
 when they first start using computers.

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:03.800
 And so we have to get this right.

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:06.040
 We have to get these little bits of text

00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:07.700
 exactly the way they are.

00:09:07.700 --> 00:09:09.600
 Computers will blow up with syntax errors.

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:11.640
 And they seem to make quite a fuss

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:13.800
 when you make the tiniest of errors.

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:14.920
 But you'll get used to that.

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:18.120
 I mean, that's because, not because you're bad

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:20.520
 or you're less than awesome.

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:23.580
 It just means the computers can't compensate

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:24.920
 when you make small mistakes.

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:26.880
 And so you got to get used to the fact

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:29.660
 that the computer is sort of intellectually

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:31.120
 not as strong as you.

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:33.240
 And so it gets confused really easy.

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:34.980
 Even though when it gets confused,

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:36.920
 it says seemingly mean things to you.

00:09:36.920 --> 00:09:38.280
 So you'll get used to that.

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:42.140
 Okay, so the first thing I want to do

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:43.480
 is I want to throw up some text.

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:46.040
 And I want you to, while this text is up,

00:09:46.040 --> 00:09:49.800
 I want you to count the number of each word in this text

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:54.480
 and tell me what the most common word is in this text.

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:55.320
 Okay?

00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:56.160
 So here we go.

00:09:56.160 --> 00:09:58.580
 (soft music)

00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:09.340
 (soft music)

00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:17.840
 Okay, so I kind of made that hard on you on purpose

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:20.680
 by moving around and distracting you and confusing you.

00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:23.320
 But even if it's not moving at all,

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:25.920
 it's a little bit tricky to do.

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:28.240
 You probably stare at it a couple of times.

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:30.640
 Your brain is going back and forth and back and forth.

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:33.600
 And so text analysis is one of the great things

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:36.160
 that computers are very, very good at.

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:39.760
 And some of the things that they can translate text.

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:41.840
 And that's because they've looked at a lot of information.

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:43.360
 So looking at text is actually something

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:45.040
 computers are really good at.

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:48.720
 And so if we take a look at the kind of programs

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:51.640
 that we're going to write to do this kind of thing,

00:10:51.640 --> 00:10:54.200
 this is something that humans are not naturally good at,

00:10:54.200 --> 00:10:55.640
 but computers are super good at.

00:10:55.640 --> 00:10:59.400
 Now, I'm not going to have you look at this code.

00:10:59.400 --> 00:11:02.300
 I'm not going to, this code you will understand in a few weeks.

00:11:02.300 --> 00:11:05.360
 But basically this is a set of instructions

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:08.360
 to open a file, read that file,

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:10.360
 read all the words in the file,

00:11:10.360 --> 00:11:13.320
 create a histogram of all the words in the file,

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:15.600
 and then search through that histogram

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:18.120
 to find the most common word and tell us

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:21.140
 what the most common word is in the file.

00:11:21.140 --> 00:11:24.000
 And in this clown file, the word the is the most common.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:25.200
 It happened seven times.

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:28.120
 And here's another large file called words.txt,

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:30.820
 and the word to is the most common thing.

00:11:30.820 --> 00:11:32.680
 And our goal is to get to the point

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:34.720
 where you can write this on your own.

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:36.000
 So you can say, you know what?

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:37.560
 I got a problem to solve.

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:40.080
 That is, what's the most common word in this file?

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:43.400
 I know how to start, and then I know how to finish.

00:11:43.400 --> 00:11:45.240
 I know how to do the stuff in the middle.

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:47.980
 And we have to learn this kind of weird language,

00:11:47.980 --> 00:11:51.760
 but when we do, we can count millions of words

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:53.800
 as easily as you count 20 words.

00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:56.320
 So that's the fun of all of this,

00:11:56.320 --> 00:11:58.340
 is to teach you this language

00:11:58.340 --> 00:12:00.560
 so that you can solve that problem

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:02.040
 so that you don't have to solve it.

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:03.520
 'Cause you could solve it,

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:06.360
 but it's not something that you're naturally good at,

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:07.620
 and it's hard work.

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:10.160
 So up next, we're going to talk a little bit

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:12.080
 about the hardware architecture

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:15.560
 that you're going to be experiencing as you write programs.

00:12:15.560 --> 00:12:19.860
 (light music)

